        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Walking on Sealed Pavers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-long-before-you-can-walk-on-sealed-pavers/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long before you can walk on sealed pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "About 24 hours for light foot traffic in normal conditions, meaning around 20 degrees with moderate humidity and reasonable airflow. Add 50 percent in cool or humid weather, and up to double for a shaded, enclosed, still patio. Touch-dry at one to four hours is not the same milestone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long before I can put furniture back on sealed pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Forty-eight to 72 hours, longer than for walking, because furniture feet apply a concentrated point load that does not move and will dent a film that is walkable but still soft. Use pads under narrow metal or plastic feet even after that, particularly on a wet-look film."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long before driving on sealed pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Seventy-two hours minimum, and longer in cool or humid conditions. Driving straight over is less demanding than turning the wheels while stationary, which applies enormous shear through power steering and will lift and scar a film that has not reached full hardness."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between dry and cured paver sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Drying is the carrier leaving — water or solvent evaporating — and it proceeds from the surface downward, which is why the top feels dry while material below is still soft. Curing is the film reaching its final structure and hardness, which continues for days to weeks after it is dry to the touch."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens if it rains on freshly sealed pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rain on an uncured film can cause blushing, spotting and a patchy matte finish, and on a water-based product it can wash material off. The risk window is typically 4 to 12 hours for water-based and 6 to 24 for solvent. Do not strip it immediately — many blushes clear over several warm dry days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealer cure slower in cold weather?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Substantially. Below about 10 degrees Celsius a water-based film may not coalesce properly at all, producing a weak film that fails early rather than simply curing slowly. Sealing late in the season with mild days and cold nights is a common way to get a poor result from a perfectly good product."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How can I tell if paver sealer is fully cured?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Press a thumbnail firmly into the film in a hidden corner: any impression means it is still soft. After 24 hours, walk a concealed section in clean soft-soled shoes and check for marking in raking light. Do not use a water test — beading appears long before the film has hardened."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
