    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A sealer that feels dry underfoot after four hours may still be soft enough to take a permanent footprint, a pot ring or a tyre mark at twelve. Touch is a poor test and it is the only one most people use.
        </p>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Timeline
          </h2>

          <p>
            Figures for typical conditions, meaning around 20 degrees Celsius, moderate humidity and reasonable airflow. Every one of them extends in cool, humid or shaded conditions.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Time</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Touch dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface no longer tacky; film still soft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Careful walking in soft shoes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Normal foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ordinary use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replace furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48-72 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feet will dent a softer film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicles on a driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 hours minimum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Turning tyres are the real test</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full chemical cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-30 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Product dependent; avoid harsh cleaning</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Safe from rain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-12 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check the product; some want 24</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row people get wrong most often is furniture. A patio set replaced at 24 hours can leave permanent dents and ring marks where the feet sit, because the film is walkable but still soft enough to deform under a concentrated point load that never moves. Wait the full 48 to 72 hours, and put coasters or pads under narrow feet even then.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vehicle row matters on a driveway and is routinely underestimated. Driving straight over a 48-hour film may be fine; turning the wheels while stationary on it will lift and scar it. Power steering on a parked car applies enormous shear to a surface, and that is the load a curing film handles worst.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always check your specific product, because these vary meaningfully between formulations and the manufacturer's figures assume their own test conditions. Where the tin and this page disagree, the tin wins.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">24 hours to walk, 48 for furniture, 72 for vehicles — and add 50 percent in cool or humid weather.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Touch-dry is not a milestone worth acting on. The film continues hardening long after the surface stops feeling tacky, and the marks you make in that window — footprints, pot rings, tyre scars — do not buff out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="dry-vs-cured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Dry Is Not Cured, and the Difference Is the Whole Point
          </h2>

          <p>
            Understanding the two processes explains why the timeline looks the way it does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying is the carrier leaving. For a water-based sealer that means water evaporating; for a solvent-based one it means the solvent evaporating. It happens from the surface downward, which is why the top feels dry while the material underneath is still full of carrier. This is what people test when they touch it, and it is the less important of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Curing is the film reaching its final structure. In a water-based product that means the acrylic particles coalescing and fusing into a continuous film, which continues well after the water has gone. In a solvent-based one the resin continues to harden and cross-link. Either way, the film's hardness, adhesion and chemical resistance keep developing for days and sometimes weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            So a film can be entirely dry to the touch and only a fraction of the way to its final hardness. Walking on it at that point does not smear it, but it can deform it, press grit into it, and leave marks that become permanent as it finishes hardening.
          </p>

          <p>
            This also explains why the vehicle wait is so much longer than the walking wait. Foot traffic is a distributed, brief load. A tyre turning under a stationary car is a concentrated, sustained shear load, and a film at 70 percent of its final hardness handles the first easily and the second badly.
          </p>

          <p>
            And it explains the chemical resistance caveat. Avoid harsh cleaners, de-icing salt and anything aggressive for the first few weeks, because the film's resistance to them is the last property to develop fully.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-slows" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Slows Curing Down
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four variables, and most patios have at least one working against them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Temperature</h3>

          <p>
            The biggest factor. Curing chemistry slows markedly as temperature falls, and below about 10 degrees Celsius a water-based film may not coalesce properly at all, producing a weak film that fails early rather than simply curing slowly. Sealing late in the season, when days are mild but nights are cold, is a common way to get a poor result from a good product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Humidity</h3>

          <p>
            High humidity slows evaporation, which delays the drying phase and pushes everything back. It hits water-based products harder, since they have water to lose into air already full of it. A humid, still day can double the time before foot traffic is safe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shade and Airflow</h3>

          <p>
            A shaded, sheltered patio cures considerably slower than an open sunny one, because both warmth and air movement are reduced. A north-facing courtyard enclosed by walls is the slowest case, and the same product that is walkable in 24 hours on an open terrace may want 48 there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film Thickness</h3>

          <p>
            A heavy coat takes disproportionately longer, because the carrier at the bottom has further to travel and the surface skins over first and traps it. This is the same mechanism that causes blushing, and it is the main reason two thin coats beat one thick one, as our guide on how to seal pavers covers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">How Much Conditions Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">+50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cool or humid day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+100%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Shaded, still, humid</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Under 10°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water-based may not cure at all</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">One thick coat vs two thin</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="water-vs-solvent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. By Sealer Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two carriers behave differently enough to be worth separating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers are generally touch-dry faster, often within one to two hours in good conditions, because water evaporates readily from a thin film. But full cure depends on coalescence, which is strongly temperature-dependent, so the gap between touch-dry and genuinely cured is wide and conditions-sensitive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based sealers take somewhat longer to reach touch-dry, typically two to four hours, because the solvent has to leave a film that has penetrated further into the surface. Their cure is less temperature-sensitive at the low end, which makes them somewhat more tolerant of a cool day, though they remain very intolerant of moisture in the pavers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Water-Based</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solvent-Based</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Touch dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold sensitivity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High — may not coalesce</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Humidity sensitivity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rain risk window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-12 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-24 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Odour during cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong for 24-48 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The odour row matters for an enclosed patio. A solvent-based sealer curing next to open windows will make the inside of a house unpleasant for a day or two, and on a balcony or courtyard that is a genuine reason to choose water-based, as our comparison of the two covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are different again and generally the fastest to return to service, often walkable within a few hours, because there is no surface film to harden. That is one of their underappreciated practical advantages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Damages a Curing Film
          </h2>

          <p>
            The specific things that leave permanent marks, in rough order of how often they happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain, first and worst. Water landing on an uncured film can cause blushing, spotting and a patchy matte finish, and on a water-based product it can wash material off entirely. Check the forecast for a genuine clear window, not just a dry afternoon, and remember that the risk window extends into the night when temperatures drop and dew forms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Irrigation is the version of rain people forget. A sprinkler timer running at six in the morning onto a patio sealed the previous afternoon will ruin it, and this happens constantly. Turn off any irrigation reaching the patio for several days, and check the zones rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Furniture feet. Narrow metal or plastic feet concentrate load onto a small area and dent a soft film, leaving permanent impressions. Wait the full 48 to 72 hours and use pads under narrow feet, particularly on a wet-look film which is softer than a matte one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pots and planters. A pot placed on a curing film traps moisture underneath and leaves a ring that will not clean off. Give it a full week before anything sits permanently on the surface, and use feet under pots afterwards regardless.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paws and grit. Pets crossing a curing patio leave prints, and grit walked onto a soft film gets pressed in and stays. Keep the area closed off for the first day at minimum, which is easier said than done and worth the effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leaves and debris. Anything that lands on a curing film and sits there can leave a mark, particularly wet leaves which also hold moisture against the surface. Sealing under a tree in autumn is a scheduling problem as much as a weather one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Testing Whether It Is Ready
          </h2>

          <p>
            Better methods than pressing a finger on it and hoping.
          </p>

          <p>
            The thumbnail test. Press a thumbnail firmly into the film in an inconspicuous corner. If it leaves an impression, the film is still soft and furniture and vehicles should wait. This is more informative than touch because it tests hardness rather than surface tackiness.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inconspicuous walk. After 24 hours, walk on a hidden section in clean soft-soled shoes and look at the surface in raking light. Any marking or dulling means give it longer. Doing this in a corner rather than the middle means a mistake is not on display.
          </p>

          <p>
            The smell test for solvent products. A solvent-based sealer that still smells strongly is still off-gassing and is not fully cured. Diminishing odour tracks curing reasonably well for these, though it is not a precise measure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use a water test to judge cure. Beading appears early, well before the film has hardened, so a patio that sheds water is not necessarily a patio ready for furniture. The water test is for checking whether an old sealer has worn out, which our guide on how often to seal pavers covers, not for judging a fresh one.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, wait. Nothing is lost by giving it another day, and the marks made by rushing are permanent. This is a job where the cost of patience is zero and the cost of impatience is a patio you look at every day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The First Week
          </h2>

          <p>
            What to do and avoid while the film finishes hardening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep irrigation off the patio for at least three days, and longer if the weather is cool. This is the single most common preventable ruin of a new seal, and it takes one trip to the controller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid cleaning it at all for the first week or two. Chemical resistance is the last property to develop, so detergents, acidic cleaners and particularly de-icing salts can damage a film that looks fully cured. If something spills, blot it with water rather than reaching for a cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not pressure wash it for at least a month. A film at partial hardness will not withstand it, and there is no reason to wash a patio you sealed three weeks ago in any case.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reintroduce furniture gradually and with pads under narrow feet. Reintroduce pots last, after a full week, with feet under them so moisture is not trapped against the surface permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the appearance to settle. A wet-look film in particular can look uneven or unusually glossy for the first few days as it levels and cures, and judging the result on the evening you applied it is unfair to the product. Look at it properly after a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if something does go wrong — a rain shower at hour six, a sprinkler you forgot — do not immediately strip it. Many blushing problems resolve over several warm dry days as trapped moisture finds its way out, and a solvent-based film can often be rescued with xylene. Our guide on why paver sealer turns white covers the diagnosis before the expensive response.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Readiness by Touch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Touch-dry happens in one to four hours; the film keeps hardening for days. A surface that no longer feels tacky can still take a permanent footprint, a pot ring or a tyre scar. Use a thumbnail pressed into a hidden corner to test hardness rather than tackiness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing Furniture at 24 Hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twenty-four hours is for light foot traffic, which is a brief distributed load. Furniture feet apply a concentrated point load that never moves, and they dent a film that is walkable but still soft. Wait 48 to 72 hours and use pads under narrow feet even then.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Irrigation Timer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sprinkler zone running at six in the morning onto a patio sealed the previous afternoon is the single most common preventable ruin of a new seal. Turn off any irrigation that reaches the patio for at least three days, and check the zones rather than assuming you know which reach it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning the Wheels on a Driveway at 48 Hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driving straight across a partly cured film may be fine; turning the wheels while stationary applies enormous shear and will lift and scar it. Power steering on a parked car is the load a curing film handles worst. Seventy-two hours minimum, longer in cool weather.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning the Patio in the First Fortnight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chemical resistance is the last property a film develops, so detergents, acidic cleaners and de-icing salts can damage a surface that looks fully cured. Blot spills with water for the first week or two, and do not pressure wash a newly sealed patio for at least a month.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can walk on sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 24 hours for light foot traffic in normal conditions, meaning around 20 degrees with moderate humidity and reasonable airflow. Add 50 percent in cool or humid weather, and up to double for a shaded, enclosed, still patio. Touch-dry at one to four hours is not the same milestone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can put furniture back on sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Forty-eight to 72 hours, longer than for walking, because furniture feet apply a concentrated point load that does not move and will dent a film that is walkable but still soft. Use pads under narrow metal or plastic feet even after that, particularly on a wet-look film.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before driving on sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seventy-two hours minimum, and longer in cool or humid conditions. Driving straight over is less demanding than turning the wheels while stationary, which applies enormous shear through power steering and will lift and scar a film that has not reached full hardness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between dry and cured paver sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Drying is the carrier leaving — water or solvent evaporating — and it proceeds from the surface downward, which is why the top feels dry while material below is still soft. Curing is the film reaching its final structure and hardness, which continues for days to weeks after it is dry to the touch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains on freshly sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain on an uncured film can cause blushing, spotting and a patchy matte finish, and on a water-based product it can wash material off. The risk window is typically 4 to 12 hours for water-based and 6 to 24 for solvent. Do not strip it immediately — many blushes clear over several warm dry days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealer cure slower in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Substantially. Below about 10 degrees Celsius a water-based film may not coalesce properly at all, producing a weak film that fails early rather than simply curing slowly. Sealing late in the season with mild days and cold nights is a common way to get a poor result from a perfectly good product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can I tell if paver sealer is fully cured?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Press a thumbnail firmly into the film in a hidden corner: any impression means it is still soft. After 24 hours, walk a concealed section in clean soft-soled shoes and check for marking in raking light. Do not use a water test — beading appears long before the film has hardened.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

