        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Twenty-four hours to walk, 48 to 72 for furniture, 72 minimum for vehicles, and add half again in cool, humid or shaded conditions. Touch-dry at one to four hours is not a milestone worth acting on, because drying and curing are different processes and the film keeps hardening long after the surface stops feeling tacky.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The marks made in that gap are permanent — footprints, furniture dents, pot rings, tyre scars. Turn off the irrigation for three days, keep pets and pots off for a week, avoid cleaners and salt for a fortnight, and test hardness with a thumbnail in a hidden corner rather than a fingertip in the middle. Waiting an extra day costs nothing; rushing costs a patio you look at every day.
          </p>
        </div>

